WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.373
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:09.002
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:16.970
Unit twenty-seven: Historical Fiction and Truth. Today you will speculate about invention and accuracy with modal perfects.

4
00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.910
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:24.610
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.904
Historical fiction.

7
00:00:27.917 --> 00:00:32.865
Historical fiction means novels or films set in the past that mix fact and invention.

8
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.076
Historical fiction brings the past to life.

9
00:00:36.683 --> 00:00:39.777
Your turn. Say: Historical fiction.

10
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:44.029
Historical fiction.

11
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:45.889
Accuracy.

12
00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:50.706
Accuracy means the quality of being correct and precise.

13
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.560
Critics questioned the film's accuracy.

14
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.640
Your turn. Say: Accuracy.

15
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:00.466
Accuracy.

16
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:02.415
Imagination.

17
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:07.355
Imagination means the ability to create new images and ideas.

18
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.518
The author used imagination to fill gaps.

19
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:13.723
Your turn. Say: Imagination.

20
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.742
Imagination.

21
00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:19.461
Source.

22
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:23.738
Source means a document or object providing information.

23
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.930
The novel draws on diaries as its source.

24
00:01:27.517 --> 00:01:29.995
Your turn. Say: Source.

25
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:33.846
Source.

26
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:35.608
Narrative.

27
00:01:36.617 --> 00:01:39.266
Narrative means a story or account of events.

28
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:43.452
The narrative follows a family in 1975.

29
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.598
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

30
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:50.237
Narrative.

31
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:52.042
Anachronism.

32
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.826
Anachronism means something placed in the wrong historical period.

33
00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:00.345
A wristwatch in an ancient scene is an anachronism.

34
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.509
Your turn. Say: Anachronism.

35
00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:07.437
Anachronism.

36
00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:09.649
Representation.

37
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.625
Representation means the way something is portrayed.

38
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:17.760
The representation of villagers felt stereotyped.

39
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.142
Your turn. Say: Representation.

40
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.547
Representation.

41
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:27.075
Truth.

42
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:30.408
Truth means what is real or actually happened.

43
00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.248
Fiction can express emotional truth.

44
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.050
Your turn. Say: Truth.

45
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:39.599
Truth.

46
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.039
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:51.506
Visal, you read a novel about the evacuation of Phnom Penh in 1975. How accurate is it?

48
00:02:52.617 --> 00:03:01.922
The main events match historical sources. But the author may have invented this dialogue, since no one could have recorded such private conversations.

49
00:03:03.017 --> 00:03:05.051
Does that make the novel dishonest?

50
00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:16.326
Not necessarily. The characters might have spoken differently, but their fear and confusion must have been very similar to what survivors describe.

51
00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:19.161
Did you notice any errors?

52
00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:29.636
One anachronism: a character uses a mobile phone expression. The editor should have caught it, though it doesn't change the larger truth.

53
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.184
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:36.245
Grammar focus: Modal perfect for speculation.

55
00:03:36.545 --> 00:03:37.872
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:40.792
The author may have invented this dialogue.

57
00:03:41.683 --> 00:03:44.925
Their fear must have been very similar to survivors' accounts.

58
00:03:45.817 --> 00:03:49.175
No one could have recorded such private conversations.

59
00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:59.636
Modal perfects show how certain we are about the past: must have (almost certain), may / might / could have (possible), can't / couldn't have (almost impossible). 'Should have' criticises a past mistake.

60
00:04:00.383 --> 00:04:04.290
Be careful! Don't say: The author may invented this dialogue.

61
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:09.208
Say: The author may have invented this dialogue.

62
00:04:09.508 --> 00:04:12.216
Speculation: may have + p.p.

63
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.501
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.765
Facts and Feelings.

65
00:04:18.065 --> 00:04:22.724
Historical fiction occupies an uncertain space between scholarship and storytelling.

66
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:28.879
Novelists often invent dialogue and private thoughts that historians could never have documented.

67
00:04:29.867 --> 00:04:35.140
Some critics argue that such inventions may have distorted public understanding of real events.

68
00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:41.604
Others respond that fiction can convey the emotional truth of an experience in ways that archives cannot.

69
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.640
Careful readers therefore enjoy historical fiction while checking its claims against reliable historical sources.

70
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:53.840
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.683 --> 00:04:57.345
The writer blank this scene; there is no record of it.

72
00:04:57.645 --> 00:05:01.670
A, may have invented. B, may invented. C, may have invent.

73
00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:07.755
The answer is A: may have invented.

74
00:05:08.917 --> 00:05:12.066
They blank been terrified; the city was emptied in days.

75
00:05:12.366 --> 00:05:15.822
A, must have. B, must. C, should.

76
00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:21.192
The answer is A: must have.

77
00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.418
Something placed in the wrong historical period is an blank.

78
00:05:25.718 --> 00:05:30.416
A, accuracy. B, anachronism. C, imagination.

79
00:05:33.967 --> 00:05:36.407
The answer is B: anachronism.

80
00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:39.596
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:39.896 --> 00:05:46.405
A, take liberties with the facts. B, make liberties to the facts. C, do liberties of facts.

82
00:05:49.967 --> 00:05:53.127
The answer is A: take liberties with the facts.

83
00:05:54.283 --> 00:05:56.701
According to the reading, fiction can convey…

84
00:05:57.001 --> 00:06:03.650
A, only documented facts. B, the emotional truth of an experience. C, nothing reliable.

85
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.104
The answer is B: the emotional truth of an experience.

86
00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.637
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.483 --> 00:06:18.634
Can a novel teach us real history?

88
00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.066
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.366 --> 00:07:12.360
A novel can teach real history, as long as readers know what is fact and what is invention. A well-researched novel may show what people must have felt, which textbooks often fail to do. For example, novels about the Khmer Rouge period have made younger readers understand their families' suffering more deeply. However, an author may have changed dates or invented characters for dramatic effect. So I think fiction is a good starting point, but it should lead readers to historical sources.

90
00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.463
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:19.930
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal perfect for speculation.

92
00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:24.298
Today's value is truthfulness. Tell the truth and check the facts.

93
00:07:24.598 --> 00:07:27.460
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.354
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

